      "id": "551uj9n",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Factors Affecting Natural Vegetation"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Natural vegetation grows without human intervention and relies on variations in relief, soil, temperature, and rainfall.",
        "India's vegetation is classified into five major types: Tropical Evergreen, Tropical Deciduous, Thorn, Montane, and Mangrove forests.",
        "Tropical Evergreen forests remain so dense under continuous heavy rainfall and high temperatures that sunlight rarely reaches the ground.",
        "Tropical Deciduous forests are the most widespread, economically valuable, and are commonly termed monsoon forests.",
        "Mangrove forests are salt-tolerant ecosystems in tidal regions whose complex roots protect coastlines from erosion and cyclone damage."
      ],
      "id": "snkf3g3"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The distribution of natural vegetation in India depends on climatic and geographical factors.",
      "id": "y6s79qc"
    },
